#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "ccfb8833-f270-5855-8c49-fb3b6b6662d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18776" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $5^{x+2}=9^{x-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+2} & ={9}^{x-2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{x-2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(x+2)\\ln(5) = (x-2)\\ln(9).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9))}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "453d4ec3-98f5-5db1-a046-a6b15c13049d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4097" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of $\\log_2,$ what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $2^{x+1}=3^x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3^{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x \\log_2 \\left(3\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x{log}_{2}⁡(3)-x & =1 \\\\ x({log}_{2}⁡(3)-1) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{{log}_{2}⁡(3)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "fbb90aa1-5adf-5c6d-8499-68eea953cc42" :question/math-academy-id "e-4098" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $e^{x+1}=2^x\\cdot 5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1} & ={2}^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x}\\cdot 5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\ln \\left( e^a \\right) = a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 =\\ln \\left(2^{x}\\cdot 5\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbf73f4-86d1-5bfd-9e9d-5a3cfdc63e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236191} {:answer/id #uuid "3cac3a9a-26b3-5ebf-89d6-f1f0bfe75ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236198} {:answer/id #uuid "50640e0e-0e74-5967-8122-cf3f2c127102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236332} {:answer/id #uuid "6764564c-a351-5594-9622-c052823ed9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236469} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e7fc27-1a16-5599-b3af-e337af933f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6764564c-a351-5594-9622-c052823ed9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236469} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ba9cac-4726-57ff-bcea-b0fc9a870e93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd5c6b81-63d3-5a9b-b188-f8be38d27f58" :question/math-academy-id "q-100471" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${3}^{x+5}={7}^{6-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+5} & ={7}^{6-2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x+5}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{6-2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(6-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(6-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "30df98ac-26e2-575f-9740-2cdd94af54be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178420}] :question/id #uuid "3a4d2c64-ecf2-540e-86cd-1d47fbb20b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-233243" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $e^{3x + 1} = 2^{x - 1}$.$x =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36d18ca3-aa8a-5c2b-8ba9-858b796a780c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}(6)}{3-2\\operatorname{ln}(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36d18ca3-aa8a-5c2b-8ba9-858b796a780c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}(6)}{3-2\\operatorname{ln}(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288399} :answer-field/id #uuid "1991aa2c-1472-5830-92f9-81b17a0bd32f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1285c9cd-7326-5f21-8cc4-238b71cf12c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-242903" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{3x-1}={6}^{2x+1}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x-1} & ={6}^{2x+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-1=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2x+1}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n3x-1=(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 3x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x(3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}{3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de8c4e1d-1932-5465-80c5-a8d6e9673b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}(6)-\\operatorname{ln}(5)}{4\\operatorname{ln}(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8c4e1d-1932-5465-80c5-a8d6e9673b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}(6)-\\operatorname{ln}(5)}{4\\operatorname{ln}(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289512} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a7b683d-9fcf-5ed1-8675-d32910b910e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c423a638-983b-5ebe-b413-4d69284f1a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-242919" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${5}^{4x+1}={6}^{7x+5}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{4x+1} & ={6}^{7x+5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{4x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{7x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)=(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f85746-b79b-5580-b5d4-13f1d8e44462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.293" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290737} {:answer/id #uuid "33cde5e3-9a3b-53ea-b943-70c8a99494f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.891" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290840} {:answer/id #uuid "35d82a02-03b6-51c5-8449-78b2bc84ce9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290852} {:answer/id #uuid "4a79d869-63a0-57b2-96e1-c9779852a986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.712" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290948} {:answer/id #uuid "fc971f9e-6d7d-5373-b123-f07b18462734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.544" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d82a02-03b6-51c5-8449-78b2bc84ce9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290852} :answer-field/id #uuid "191c3242-0209-5594-87e0-47ca785c4d4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d6a09c3-0907-53a4-ad37-e4629c10f3e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-26828" :question/problem "Given that ${5}^{y}={4}^{y+1},$ what is the value of $y?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{5}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{y}={4}^{y+1} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{y}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({4}^{y+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{5}⁡({4}^{y+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(y+1){log}_{5}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(y+1){log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y & =y{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y-y{log}_{5}⁡4 & ={log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y(1-{log}_{5}⁡4) & ={log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡4}{1-{log}_{5}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡(4)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}≈0.861\\,353.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, we can approximate $y$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y≈\\frac{0.861\\,353}{1-0.861\\,353}≈6.213,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places."}]}
